analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment in set method.

We have class RoleList extends ArrayList<Object>. The method set overrides set(int index, Object element). The comment: "If the index is within the valid range of the list, then for every index i less than the specified index, the element at i remains unchanged or continues to be null after the operation."

We need to express: (index in valid range) => for all i, 0 <= i < index, the element at i after operation equals the element before operation, or both are null? It says "remains unchanged or continues to be null". That means after operation, either same as before, or null (if it was null before?). Actually "remains unchanged or continues to be null" could be interpreted as: after operation, the element at i is equal to the old element at i, or the old element at i was null (so it continues to be null). But "remains unchanged or continues to be null" maybe means if it was null before, it stays null; if it was non-null, it stays same. That's just equality: this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))? But need to handle null safely: we can use == for null, or .equals? In spec examples they used .equals for objects. They used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) for array elements. For possible null, .equals would throw NPE. Better to express: (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But spec may allow equality with == for objects? In JML, you can use \old(this.get(i)) == this.get(i) but that checks reference equality. Might be okay.

Simplify: assert (0 <= index && index < this.size()) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

But need to handle null: we could use (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But spec examples didn't handle null. Might be okay to just use equals assuming non-null. The comment includes "or continues to be null". So equality covers that case (null equals null). If we use .equals, null would cause NPE. So better to use == for null case. Use expression: (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Thus final assert:

assert (0 <= index && index < this.size()) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

